AutorAssessmentListener.java

{{String assessmentTitle = TextFormat.convertPlaintextToFormattedTextNoHighUnicode(author.getAssessTitle());}}

The problems lies in …convertPlainTextToFormattedTextNoHighUnicode(…) Function in TextFormat.java

that replaces the chars

{noformat}case '<':
{
 if (buf == null) buf = new StringBuilder(value.substring(0, i));
 buf.append("&lt;");
}
break;{noformat}



a possible quickfix is to replace them in PublishedAssessmentSettingsBean.java like:

{noformat}// Original title with potential HTML entities
String encodedTitle = assessment.getTitle();

// Unapply the encoding to revert to the original characters
String unencodedTitle = encodedTitle
        .replace("&amp;", "&")
        .replace("&lt;", "<")
        .replace("&gt;", ">")
        .replace("&quot;", "\"")
        .replace("<br />\n", "\n");

// Use the unencoded title for category selection
this.categorySelected = getCategoryForAssessmentName(unencodedTitle);{noformat}

I tested this manually putting an LTI 1.3 tool in home and it worked with the latest patch. You can also show that the Base64Urlencode never adds a plus by taking your string:

{{/access/lti/site/~e9376efc-2102-4eb8-ac60-0e7ba383cb50/content:118}}

And using [https://www.base64encode.org/|https://www.base64encode.org/|smart-link]  to encode it without and with url encoding. You see a + in the non-url encoding and not in url safe encoding. The plus comes out because of the “~” and the position of the “~” - but with our URL naming conventions (after the lti rename) - the ~ always ends up in position 17


And [user] to answer your question w.r.t. 22 and 23 - it is not a problem - only because the url pattern is different before the basiclti → lti rename 🙂  If you encode with base 64 non-url encoding, the “~” is in a different position and no “+” comes out:

{{/access/basiclti/site/~e9376efc-2102-4eb8-ac60-0e7ba383cb50/content:118}}

Kind of crazy - a great bug to find and fix.

