Hi Sam,

Thank you for taking the time to review this and for the detailed explanation.

I realize our original ticket summary and description may have been misleading by referencing {{BaseAuthzGroupService}} rather than the in-memory domain object {{BaseAuthzGroup}}. You are completely right that {{DbAuthzGroupService.getUsersIsAllowed}} and {{getAuthzGroupsIsAllowed}} already perform bulk database reads with {{IN}} clauses at the storage layer.

The actual N+1 bottleneck we encountered occurs in {{BaseAuthzGroup.java}} (the in-memory group/realm instance), specifically triggered by {{isRoleViewType()}} checks against {{UserDirectoryService}}.

h4. 1. The Call Chain and Root Cause

In {{BaseAuthzGroup.java}}, methods like {{getUsers()}}, {{getMembers()}}, {{getUsersIsAllowed(lock)}}, and {{getUsersHasRole(role)}} filter the active user grants using a stream:

{code:java}// BaseAuthzGroup.java (lines ~810-855)
return m_userGrants.entrySet().stream()
    .filter(e -> e.getValue().isActive() 
            && !userDirectoryService.isRoleViewType(e.getKey())) // <-- The issue originates here
    .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
    .collect(Collectors.toSet());{code}

Tracing {{userDirectoryService.isRoleViewType(id)}} into {{BaseUserDirectoryService.java}}:

{code:java}// BaseUserDirectoryService.java (line 245)
[user]
public boolean isRoleViewType(String id) {
    if (id == null) return false;
    return getOptionalUser(id).map(u -> ROLEVIEW_USER_TYPE.equals(u.getType())).orElse(false);
}{code}

{{isRoleViewType(id)}} delegates to {{getOptionalUser(id)}}. 

h4. 2. Why N+1 Queries Occur in Large Sites

In university-wide mega-courses (e.g., in our production environment we have single course sites with 4,000 to 4,500+ enrolled students):

* Whenever {{BaseAuthzGroup.getUsers()}} or {{getUsersIsAllowed()}} is invoked on a *cold cache* (e.g., following a server restart, cache eviction under memory pressure, or when accessing an uncached course), {{m_userGrants}} contains 4,000+ entries.
* As the stream evaluates {{!userDirectoryService.isRoleViewType(e.getKey())}} for each student, each cache miss results in an individual query:
{code:sql}SELECT ... FROM SAKAI_USER WHERE USER_ID = ?{code}
* This produces *$N$ individual database roundtrips* ($N$ = number of site participants) sequentially during a single request.

h4. 3. How the PR Solves It

The patch in commit {{2025b3f7c9c}} adds a bulk cache pre-warm immediately before the stream filter executes:

{code:java}if (!m_userGrants.isEmpty()) {
    userDirectoryService.getUsers(m_userGrants.keySet());
}{code}

{{userDirectoryService.getUsers(Collection<String> userIds)}} issues a single batched SQL query ({{WHERE USER_ID IN (...)}}) and populates the {{UserDirectoryService}} memory cache. Consequently, all 4,000+ subsequent {{isRoleViewType()}} calls in the stream result in immediate in-memory cache hits, reducing 4,000+ individual queries down to a single bulk query.

----

Could you please reconsider reopening this issue with this clarified scope? If helpful, I would be more than happy to provide:

# Hibernate/MySQL SQL trace logs demonstrating the sequential {{SAKAI_USER}} queries before and after the change.
# A reproducible unit/integration test demonstrating the cold-cache behavior.

Thank you again for your guidance!

